\section{Abstract}
The objective of this project is to analyze a large social network graph from GitHub, obtained from the MUSAE project. The dataset comprises 37,700 nodes, each representing developers who have starred at least ten repositories, and 289,003 undirected edges corresponding to mutual follower relationships. Each node is labeled as either a Machine Learning Engineer or a Web Developer, based on the individual's job title. This study will employ graph analysis techniques to investigate the relationships within these graphs.
GitHub is a widely used platform for hosting and sharing open-source software. Many computer programmers maintain a GitHub profile. The platform provides an accessible and cost-effective way to share code internationally. In professions such as Software Engineering and Data Science, recruiters and hiring managers often ask candidates for access to their GitHub profiles, viewing them as portfolios of publicly available projects.
\subsection{Prior Work in the same Field}
The dataset that I used came from \href{https://snap.stanford.edu/data/github-social.html}{MUSAE Dataset Collection} on \href{https://snap.stanford.edu}{Stanford Network Analysis Project}. In the paper accompanying the project, the authors introduced an attribute, MUSAE, for learning node embeddings. In this study, they demonstrated that the MUSAE method outperformed contemporary methods for node classification tasks \cite{rozemberczki2021musae}.
\subsection{Limitations}
The limitations of this dataset are that it is a snapshot of the developer community from June 2019. That means the data is quite outdated and will likely require new data in the near future to be more useful. Also, there are many more titles than just Machine Learning Engineer or Web Developer, so it's not the most ideal way to represent the data.
\section{Data}
\subsection{Source and Creation}
This dataset originates from the MUSAE project within the Stanford SNAP collection. The authors collected developer information using the GitHub API in June 2019. In this dataset, nodes represent developers with at least 10 starred repositories, and edges indicate mutual follower relationships. An additional subset of the dataset provides information on user location, employer, starred repositories, and node classification by job title. For this project, each node is labeled as either Web Developer (0) or Machine Learning Engineer (1).

\subsection{About the Dataset}
After a brief analysis of the data, I realized that we are looking at a simple connected network of 37700 developers and their mutual followers (edges in this case). The data is in two files: the first is the target file, which contains node IDs, names, and labels, and is the most important file for this project. The second file is the edgelist, which lists the start and end nodes for each edge.
\section{Methods and Results}
We constructed an undirected graph from the edgelist using the igraph package in R and confirmed it is a simple graph with no self-loops or multiple edges. Following the methodology in Kolaczyk and Csardi~\cite{kolaczyk2020sand} (Chapters 2--6), we conducted sequential analysis using the loaded igraph network object.
