\subsection{Vertex Centrality}
Vertex centrality measures the importance of individual nodes in a network. Four centrality measures available in the igraph package for R will be plotted. Due to the network's size, visualizations may be difficult to interpret, but the analysis will proceed as planned.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption{Summary Statistics of Network Centrality Measures}
  \begin{tabular}{l|rrrr}
    \hline
    Statistic & Degree       & Betweenness & Closeness & Eigenvector \
    \hline
    Minimum     & 0.00002653  & 0.000000001  & 0.1414    & 0.0000000   \
    1st Quartile  & 0.00005305  & 0.000000045 & 0.2841    & 0.0004698  \
    Median   & 0.0001592   & 0.000001529 & 0.3113    & 0.0030462  \
    Mean     & 0.0004067   & 0.000059592 & 0.3137    & 0.0066687  \
    3rd Quartile  & 0.0003448   & 0.000017282 & 0.3469    & 0.0089577  \
    Maximum     & 0.2509689   & 0.269626963   & 0.5231    & 1.0000000  \
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

Now, from this table, we can get a lot of information about the graph. Looking at the \textbf{degree centrality}, the maximum value (0.25) is about (600) times the mean value (0.0004067), which means that there are a few nodes that are connected to most of the nodes in the network. On top of that, the \textbf{eigenvector centrality} has a maximum value of (1.0000000) and its mean is (0.0066687), which means that not only are the very well-connected nodes connected to other nodes at random, but they are also connected to other nodes that are also very well-connected. Now for \textbf{betweenness centrality}, the maximum value is (0.269626963), and their mean is almost zero, which means that most nodes are dead-end. However, some nodes with extremely high betweenness centrality are bridges in the graph, meaning they connect different communities. These are the choke points in a network from an economics perspective. Lastly, the \textbf{closeness centrality} has a maximum value of (0.5231), and its mean is (0.3137), which are relatively close to each other compared to the other ones, which implies that the network might be large, but it's very tight-knit.

Now, I will plot the correlation between the centrality measures to see if there is any relationship.
