  \caption{Correlation between centrality measures in the GitHub network}
  \label{fig:centrality_correlation}
\end{figure}

From this correlation plot, we can see that most centrality measures are correlated with each other.
\subsection{Component Structure}
In this network, there is a single giant component that encompasses all nodes and edges.
\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
    \hline
    \textbf{Components} & \textbf{Nodes} & \textbf{Edges} & \textbf{Articulation Points}\
    \hline
    1 & 37,700 & 289,003 & 350 \
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Properties of the giant component in the GitHub network.}
\end{table}
So this network is a single giant component that has tons of nodes and edges. But the interesting part is that there are only 350 articulation points, meaning that if we remove those nodes and their edges, the graph would become disconnected. This network is not very robust or resilient to failures because it uses a hub-and-spoke architecture.
\subsection{Transitivity and Clustering}

From the previous section, we realized that this network contains a single connected component and is neither very transitive nor resilient to failures. So that means that we can find a community structure in this network. But before that, we need to talk about local and global transitivity, as it will give us a better understanding of the network.
\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
    \hline
    \textbf{Network} & \textbf{Global} & \textbf{Local} \
    \hline
    GitHub Network & 0.01235719 & 0.1934205 \
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Transitivity of the GitHub network.}
\end{table}

Since transitivity is the probability that two nodes are connected given that they are connected to a third node, the GitHub network is not very transitive. Therefore, the conclusion is that although this graph might be a single giant component, it's not very well connected, as developers, on average, don't know each other very well. That is somewhat tied to the real work on GitHub because, as a developer myself, I don't think I really know many other developers who are on GitHub. The only ones I follow are the most popular ones, and just like me, most developers follow them, creating a weak community structure.
