\subsection{Assortativity}
\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lr}
    \hline
    \textbf{Assortativity} & \textbf{Value} \ \hline
    Degree & $-0.075$ \
    ML_target & 0.378 \ \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Assortativity of the GitHub network}
  \label{tab:assortativity}
\end{table}
The negative degree assortativity ($r \approx -0.075$) indicates that high-degree hubs tend to connect to low-degree nodes (disassortative mixing). The positive ML_target assortativity ($r \approx 0.378$) reveals strong homophily: developers of the same type preferentially connect.
\subsection{Comparison with Random Graphs}
In Chapter 5, we will compare the graph properties of the random graph with those of the GitHub network.
\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{l|rrrr}
    \hline
    \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Mean Degree} & \textbf{SD Degree} & \textbf{Transitivity} & \textbf{Components} \
    \hline
    GitHub Network & 15.3317 & 80.7881 & 0.0124 & 1 \
    Erd\H{o}s–R'enyi       & 15.3338 & 3.9162  & 0.0004 & 1 \
    Config Model      & 15.3317 & 80.7881 & 0.0123 & 1 \
    Watts–Strogatz    & 14.0000 & 1.1703  & 0.5052 & 1 \
    Barab'asi–Albert   & 15.9981 & 19.8770 & 0.0027 & 1 \
    \hline
    %% Bipartite
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Comparison of network properties between the GitHub network and various random graph models.}
  \label{tab:model_comparison}
\end{table}

From the table above, the Erd\H{o}s–R'enyi, Watts–Strogatz, and Barab'asi–Albert models each fail to reproduce the observed degree distribution: ER and WS produce nearly constant degrees, while BA generates a much narrower spread than the real network. The configuration model, by construction, preserves the exact degree sequence, so its mean and standard deviation of degree match the observed values. Its transitivity is also close to the observed value, which raises the question: Is the GitHub network distinguishable from a configuration model at all?

\subsubsection{Configuration Model: Path Length and Modularity}

To answer this, we generated 20 configuration-model random graphs (each preserving the observed degree sequence) and measured the average path length and modularity (fast-greedy) of each. Table~\ref{tab:cfg_comparison} reports the means and standard deviations across these 20 replicates, alongside the observed values and (z)-scores.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{l|rrrrr}
    \hline
    \textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Observed} & \textbf{Config Mean} & \textbf{Config SD} & \textbf{$z$-score} & \textbf{$p$-value} \
    \hline
    Avg Path Length & 3.26 & — & — & — & — \
    Modularity      & — & — & — & — & — \
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Observed network vs.\ configuration model (20 replicates). Values will be filled after re-running the analysis. A $z$-score far from zero (and $p < 0.05$) indicates the observed network is distinguishable from the configuration model on that metric.}
  \label{tab:cfg_comparison}
\end{table}

