The configuration model preserves the degree distribution but uniformly rewires edges subject to that constraint. If the observed network's modularity is significantly higher than the configuration model's, this means the community structure in the real network is not simply an artifact of the degree distribution—there is genuine assortative clustering beyond what degree heterogeneity alone produces. Similarly, if the average path length differs, the real network's routing structure departs from what a random degree-preserving rewiring would produce.

\subsection{Small-World Assessment}
To evaluate the small-world properties of the network, we compare the clustering coefficient and average path length of the GitHub network with those of an equivalent Erd\H{o}s–R'enyi (ER) random graph. The clustering coefficient for the GitHub network is (0.0124), which is approximately (30.2) times the ER mean of (0.000409). In contrast, the average path length is (3.30), which is comparable to the ER mean of (4.13) (a ratio of (0.80)). Since the network exhibits clustering far exceeding that of random graphs while maintaining a similar average path length, there is strong evidence for a small-world structure.

\section{Discussion and Future Work} I tried Hierarchical Clustering to find communities in the network, but it didn't work well because the network is too large, so we couldn't see all the details we didn't have.e. Also, in Chapter 6 of Kolaczyk and Csardi \cite{kolaczyk2020sand}, the authors explained Exponential Random Graph Models, Stochastic Block Models, and Latent Network Models, and how I can fit my graph to those random graph models. So, it would be nice to take a look at our graph and how that might fit into these models. Since we have to run (1000+) simulations for that one, I wasn't able to experiment with it.

I think some form of clustering would be well-suited to this project. In~\cite{kolaczyk2020sand}, we discussed hierarchical clustering of these graph networks, but implementing it on such a large graph is almost impossible, so I used a spectral clustering method to find communities in the network. One very important factor that I would like to explore is storytelling and problem-solving. So far, as I have worked with the data, I've created many visualizations but haven't quite found a use case for them. I would really be curious to see if I can start with a question and try to answer it using the data. In some ways, I would like to explore data-driven storytelling, decision-making, and problem-solving.

